 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the Westchester county administrative code, in relation to the\nlease or sale of real property for park purposes",
    "basePrintNo": "S5041",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5041-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 29,
       "fullName": "José M. Serrano",
       "imgName": "373_jose_m._serrano.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 373,
       "sessionMemberId": 1192,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "SERRANO"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§249.101 & 249.111, Chap 852 of 1948",
    "lawSection": "Westchester County Administrative Code",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5041\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the Westchester county administrative\ncode, in relation to the lease or sale of real property for park\npurposes\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend the sections of the Westchester County Administrative Code\nrelated to the lease or sale of real property for park purposes, in\norder to prevent the county from alienating parkland without action by\nthe State Legislature.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one strikes subdivision two of section 249.101 of the Westches-\nter County Administrative Code. This subdivision states that the Board\nof Acquisition and Contract, upon the recommendation of the board, may\nauthorize the sale of any building, improvement and other materials\nwithin the boundary line of the lands so taken and belonging to the\ncounty, which the board shall certify is not required for the purposes\nof this chapter, and the proceeds of such leases and sales shall be\ndeposited with the Commissioner of Finance to the credit of the county.\n\nSection two amends section 249.111 of the Westchester County Administra-\ntive Code, to provide that for parcels under two acres and not used as\nparks, the county, upon the recommendation of the board, is hereby\nauthorized to sell and convey, by either public or private sale, any\nreal estate which may have been acquired by purchase, condemnation or\notherwise for park purposes, and which is determined by the County Board\nto be no longer required for such purposes, or to grant rights or inter-\nests in, over, under and across any real estate acquired for park\npurposes, such sale and conveyance or grant to be in accordance with the\nprovisions of the County Charter and Administrative Code.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder current state law, parkland can only be alienated through legisla-\ntive action. Westchester County is unique from other jurisdictions in\nthat a sale of parklands is essentially possible without action by the\nState Legislature, pursuant to the county's Administrative Code. The\nadoption of Westchester's Administrative Code in 1961 removed the\nprotections for parklands set out in our state law.  According to the\nCounty Attorney, a sale proposed by the County Executive could have been\npossible with simply a two-thirds vote of t he Westchester County Board\nof Legislators and a statement that the land in question was no longer\nneeded for park purposes.  This legislation would ensure that Westches-\nter County would only retain this ability for parcels under two acres\nwhich are not used a s parks. It amends sections 249.101 and 249.111 of\nthe Westchester County Administrative Code, which the County Attorney\nbelieved would have given the county the authority to complete the sale\nproposed by the Executive, as well as any future sales of parklands.\n\nWestchester County must be aligned with the other jurisdictions in the\nstate and should not be able to sell parklands without the approval of\nthe State Legislature. If the sale of parking lots as proposed was in\nfact executed, this would set a very dangerous precedent and could mean\nthat other areas deemed as parkland could be next.  Amending these rele-\nvant sections of the Westchester Administrative Code will protect the\ncounty's precious parkland from future threats.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
